Goal:
- Implement the Template Method design pattern for sorting.
- Create an abstract SortAlgorithm with a template method sort() that delegates element comparisons and swaps to abstract methods compare() and swap().
- Provide concrete subclasses BubbleSort and SelectionSort.
- Test by sorting integer and string arrays with each algorithm.

Plan and checkpoints:
- Checkpoint 1: Define abstract SortAlgorithm with the template method sort() and abstract compare()/swap().
- Checkpoint 2: Implement BubbleSort using the base class’ sort() skeleton.
- Checkpoint 3: Implement SelectionSort by overriding sort() with a selection-sort skeleton, still using compare()/swap().
- Checkpoint 4: Add a test harness that sorts integer and string arrays with both algorithms and prints results.
- Pitfalls to avoid: Ensure generics use Comparable<T>, and that the template method is clearly separated from the primitive operations.

Code (Java)

import java.util.Arrays;

public class TemplateSortDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Bubble sort with integers
        Integer[] nums = {5, 3, 8, 1, 2, 9, 0};
        BubbleSort<Integer> bubble = new BubbleSort<>();
        bubble.sort(nums);
        System.out.println("BubbleSort integers: " + Arrays.toString(nums));

        // Bubble sort with strings
        String[] words = {"banana", "apple", "cherry", "date"};
        BubbleSort<String> bubbleStr = new BubbleSort<>();
        bubbleStr.sort(words);
        System.out.println("BubbleSort strings: " + Arrays.toString(words));

        // Selection sort with integers
        Integer[] nums2 = {7, 2, 6, 4, 3, 9, 1};
        SelectionSort<Integer> sel = new SelectionSort<>();
        sel.sort(nums2);
        System.out.println("SelectionSort integers: " + Arrays.toString(nums2));

        // Selection sort with strings
        String[] words2 = {"delta","alpha","charlie","bravo"};
        SelectionSort<String> selStr = new SelectionSort<>();
        selStr.sort(words2);
        System.out.println("SelectionSort strings: " + Arrays.toString(words2));
    }
}

// Abstract class with the Template Method
abstract class SortAlgorithm<T extends Comparable<T>> {
    // Template method defining the sorting algorithm's skeleton
    public final void sort(T[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                // Primitive operation provided by subclasses
                if (compare(arr, j, j + 1) > 0) {
                    swap(arr, j, j + 1);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // Primitive operations to be implemented by subclasses
    protected abstract int compare(T[] arr, int i, int j);
    protected abstract void swap(T[] arr, int i, int j);
}

// BubbleSort uses the base skeleton
class BubbleSort<T extends Comparable<T>> extends SortAlgorithm<T> {
    @Override
    protected int compare(T[] arr, int i, int j) {
        return arr[i].compareTo(arr[j]);
    }

    @Override
    protected void swap(T[] arr, int i, int j) {
        T tmp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = tmp;
    }
}

// SelectionSort provides its own sort implementation (skeleton) using the primitives
class SelectionSort<T extends Comparable<T>> extends SortAlgorithm<T> {
    @Override
    public void sort(T[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            int minIdx = i;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (compare(arr, j, minIdx) < 0) {
                    minIdx = j;
                }
            }
            if (minIdx != i) {
                swap(arr, i, minIdx);
            }
        }
    }

    @Override
    protected int compare(T[] arr, int i, int j) {
        return arr[i].compareTo(arr[j]);
    }

    @Override
    protected void swap(T[] arr, int i, int j) {
        T tmp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = tmp;
    }
}

How it works:
- SortAlgorithm defines the template method sort() that sorts by repeatedly comparing adjacent elements and swapping when out of order. It delegates the actual comparison and swap mechanics to the abstract methods compare() and swap(), which concrete subclasses implement.
- BubbleSort provides the natural implementations of compare and swap, so it uses the base skeleton to perform a bubble-sort-like pass.
- SelectionSort overrides sort() to implement a standard selection sort skeleton, still using compare() and swap() primitives for element access and movement.

